{0 eon-edn — EDN parser}

Eon EDN is a minimal, extensible EDN reader for OCaml 5, built on
algebraic effects. It knows nothing about [eon-ecs] or [eon-engine] —
just EDN syntax in, a generic {!Eon_edn.Edn_effects.value} tree out.
Extension (custom [#tag] handling, validation, logging) is entirely a
middleware concern, layered on top of the reader without touching it.

This page is the front of a short book: read the {b Guides} below in
order for a start-to-finish walkthrough of every public feature, with
real, compiled code examples throughout (the source for every example
lives in
{{:https://github.com/imunitic/eon/tree/main/eon_edn/examples/book}[eon_edn/examples/book/]},
compiled as part of the normal build — {i not} illustrative pseudocode).
If you already know what you're looking for, jump straight to the
{b API reference} at the bottom instead.

{1 Quick start}

{[
  let v =
    Eon_edn.Edn_parser.run Eon_edn.Edn_parser.value
      {|{:name "Crab" :position [1 2] :alive true}|}
  in
  match v with
  | Eon_edn.Edn_effects.VMap _ -> print_endline "parsed a map"
  | _ -> assert false
]}

{!Eon_edn.Edn_parser.run} drives the reader over an input string and
returns the parsed {!Eon_edn.Edn_effects.value}. This is enough for plain
EDN — primitives, strings, symbols, keywords, lists [()], vectors [[]],
and maps [{}]. Tagged values ([#tag ...]) and metadata ([^meta ...]) need
one more step — see {{!page-middleware}Middleware}.

{1 Guides}

- {{!page-reading}The value type and the reader} — {!Eon_edn.Edn_effects.value},
  {!Eon_edn.Edn_parser}: the AST, the grammar entry point, and why
  [#tag]/[^meta] deliberately raise when driven via [run] alone.
- {{!page-middleware}Middleware} — {!Eon_edn.Edn_middleware}: resolving
  tags, registering handlers, composing [log_tags]/[strict_tags], and a
  worked example converting a parsed value into a typed OCaml record.

